{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ועבדתם שם אלהים", :translit "va'avadtem sham elohim", :gloss "and you shall serve there elohim"} {:he "אלהים אחרים", :translit "elohim acherim", :gloss "other elohim"} {:he "עכו\"ם", :translit "avodah zarah", :gloss "idolatry"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Anomaly detection: verse says 'elohim' without the expected modifier 'acherim' (other), as it appears everywhere else"}], :frame {:text "The phrase 'elohim acherim' (other gods) is the standard idiom for idolatrous worship found throughout the Torah (Exodus 20:3, Deuteronomy 5:7). The absence of 'acherim' in this verse is read as an allusion to the status of Israel in exile: the Shakhna rules that gentiles outside the Land of Israel are not suspected of idolatrous intent, for they serve their statues merely as royal protocol (Shulhan Arukh, Yoreh De'ah 148:12, citing Rashba). This principle means that certain commercial and social interactions with diaspora gentiles are treated more leniently than the same interactions with pagans in the Land of Israel.", :sources ["Exodus 20:3" "Deuteronomy 5:7" "Shulhan Arukh Yoreh De'ah 148:12"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Anomaly detection: verse says 'elohim' without the expected modifier 'acherim' (other), as it appears everywhere else", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:34:00.479797273Z", :model "glm-5.2"}